        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Concrete Driveway Sealing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete driveways forgive very little at application time. Nearly all the blotchy, peeling, or slippery results we see come from one of these four decisions.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Too Heavily</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A concrete sealer applied thick does not last longer — it traps solvent and moisture, clouds, and peels. Both penetrating and acrylic sealers are designed as thin coats. Two light passes with the second at right angles to the first gives even coverage without any of that risk.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a Damp or Recently Washed Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete holds water well after a pressure wash, and sealing over it produces a milky white haze that has to be stripped. Give the slab at least 24 hours of dry weather, and closer to 48 in humid conditions or shade, before anything goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Glossy Acrylic on a Sloped or Shaded Drive</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Film-forming sealers with a wet look are genuinely slick underfoot when wet, and shaded slabs that stay damp also grow algae on top of the film. On slopes, steps, or north-facing drives, either add an anti-slip aggregate or choose a penetrating sealer that leaves no film at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing New Concrete Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh concrete needs roughly 28 days to cure and release moisture. Seal it in the first weeks and the trapped moisture clouds the film and weakens the bond. Wait out the month — it is the difference between a sealer that lasts years and one that fails in a season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Does sealing a concrete driveway prevent cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sealing does not prevent structural cracks caused by soil movement, tree roots, or inadequate reinforcement. However, it does prevent surface deterioration — spalling, scaling, pitting, and surface cracking — caused by freeze-thaw cycles, salt damage, and water infiltration. A properly sealed driveway will maintain its surface integrity for decades, even if larger structural movement causes control joint cracks.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

